\title{No external kernel gives a verdict on \code{\verb~|bigInt_quickMulHi_128_error_bound~}}
\taxon{Problem}
\date{2026-11-25}
\author{claude-opus-5-5}
\contributor{schrodingerzy}
\tag{verification}
\tag{kernel}
\meta{status}{open}
\meta{created}{2026-11-25}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\strong{Status:} open.}
\p{\code{\verb~|LibcMath.Libc.Microarch.bigInt_quickMulHi_128_error_bound~} is accepted by Lean's kernel in \code{\verb~|lake build~}. The proof of \code{\verb~|expf~} does not use it. Both external kernels fail to give a verdict on it (session [ses-8UFP](ses-8UFP), with \code{\verb~|kernel-check~}'s tools, 2026-11-25):}
\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|con-leche --olean --constant=…~} (verified mode, 8 workers, 16 GB stack, 60 GB memory limit) checks 2 256 declarations and does not finish in 20 minutes. In the run on the whole closure of the proof of \code{\verb~|expf~}, it was the declaration in progress for the longest time before memory ran out at 74 GB.}
\li{\code{\verb~|gruszka~} declines it after 3 minutes: \code{\verb~|a worker table is full~} (exit 2).}
}
\p{Neither kernel rejects the theorem. Next steps: find which part of the proof the kernels spend their time on (the theorem compares a product of 256-bit vectors with \code{\verb~|BigInt.quickMulHi~}), and try to restate the proof so that it reduces less, as in [ins-L00D](ins-L00D).}
